ly  interesting because i you know like i  have a really high tolerance  actually for doing that sort of stuff  even though i said like it does crush my  soul  at a certain point but in another you  know like i find it very meditative to  work inside of spreadsheets i don't have  anything against spreadsheets but  but there is like  also this power  and  like  real importance to  advocating for  process automation and  getting to the point where you can see  and  make the business case for the  importance of  automating basic processes  um  and so i've got  i've i've had a lot of success in my own  career doing that sort of advocacy and  work  um but it does it does sometimes  come at the cost of then realizing the  type of organization that you're a part  of  this gene kim in the unicorn project  wrote really  you know like this these stories are  both the same both the phoenix project  and the unicorn project they're both  like  like  very fun because they start inside of  organizations that are somewhere between  bureaucratic and a little bit  pathological and so  and then they they build out from that  you watch these characters like crawl  their way  back out of these organizations and like  build a better  future for themselves  and so i read the unicorn project i  probably in  whenever it came out in 2020 or 2019  um  this  this captures like this really visceral  feeling that i'm talking about in this  scene the main character maxine is a  developer and she's trying to improve  how development work is done  on her project in this like crucial  critical way but everything she tries  fails and like  every bit of  progress she makes seems  impossible and she's like  at her wit's end and i'll do a dramatic  reading she buries her head in her hands  and silently screams down keyboard  um  if you are looking for  some sort of like  catharsis  uh i highly recommend this book um  and the phoenix phoenix is the most  important project  in the company they've spent 20